--! JavaScript itself, for the code that runs beside the compiled program:
--! a platform's runtime written in beni rather than in a sibling
--! (`docs/design/research/47-js-intrinsics-spike.md`, a spike).
--!
--! **Only a platform package or core may import this module.** Anywhere
--! else `import Js` is `js_outside_platform`: nothing here is checked, and
--! the wall that fences `foreign` fences this too (`boundary.md` §2, §4).
--!
--! **A call is compiled to the JavaScript it names and nothing else**: no
--! function is called, no value is wrapped. `get o "f"` is `o.f`,
--! `set o "f" v` is the statement `o.f = v`, `same a b` is `a === b` and
--! `call o "m" [a, b]` is `o.m(a, b)`. The declarations below are `foreign`
--! so that the checker types every use; `Js.js` holds the same operations
--! as functions, for the one use no call site covers — a declaration
--! passed as a value — and for check 2, which counts them.
--!
--! A property name is never renamed, and neither is a global.
--!
--! **This module names no core type** (2026-10-02, `boundary.md` §4.2):
--! a property or global name, a pattern, an argument list, a `Bool` and an
--! `Int` are each a type variable, and `typeOf` answers a `Value`. So it
--! imports nothing and depends on nothing, and every core module may
--! import it, `Basics` and `Char` included, with no cycle possible. A
--! string or list literal a call writes in place — a property name, an
--! argument list — mints no edge to `String` or `List`
--! (`static-dispatch-spike.md` §6.8).

--| Any JavaScript value, opaque to beni: `from` makes one of any beni
--| value and `to` reads one back, and neither checks anything.
pub foreign type Value


--| `null`.
pub foreign pure null : Value


--| `undefined`.
pub foreign pure undefined : Value


--| The value itself, seen as a JavaScript value. Compiled to nothing.
pub foreign pure from : returned a → Value


--| The JavaScript value read back as whatever the caller says it is.
--| Compiled to nothing, and unchecked: the caller answers for the type.
pub foreign pure to : returned Value → a


-- Identity is observed without retaining or editing either operand. The two
-- types may differ, as when Task compares a typed result with its sentinel.
--| `a === b`.
pub foreign pure same : unused a, unused b → fresh bool


--| `v === null`.
pub foreign pure isNull : unused Value → fresh bool


--| `v === undefined`.
pub foreign pure isUndefined : unused Value → fresh bool


--| `v == null`: null or undefined.
pub foreign pure isNullish : unused Value → fresh bool


--| `a < b`: JavaScript's comparison, written in place. Two numbers compare
--| by value and two strings by their UTF-16 code units. Hand it nothing
--| else: two objects compare as their text (`le` and `ge` are both true of
--| two records), and a null-prototype object or a `Symbol` throws
--| `TypeError` (`boundary.md` §4.2, *The comparisons*).
pub foreign pure lt : unused a, unused a → fresh bool


--| `a > b`.
pub foreign pure gt : unused a, unused a → fresh bool


--| `a <= b`.
pub foreign pure le : unused a, unused a → fresh bool


--| `a >= b`.
pub foreign pure ge : unused a, unused a → fresh bool


--| `a & b`.
pub foreign pure bitAnd : int, int → int


--| `a | b`.
pub foreign pure bitOr : int, int → int


--| `a ^ b`.
pub foreign pure bitXor : int, int → int


--| `a << n`.
pub foreign pure shiftLeft : int, int → int


--| `a >> n`.
pub foreign pure shiftRight : int, int → int


--| `a >>> n`: unsigned, so its answer is 0…4294967295.
pub foreign pure shiftRightZero : int, int → int


--| `a % b`: the remainder, with the sign of `a`; `NaN` when `b` is `0`.
pub foreign pure rem : int, int → int


--| `typeof v`: `"string"`, `"number"`, `"object"` and the rest, as a
--| `Value` to compare with `same`.
pub foreign pure typeOf : unused Value → fresh Value


--| `typeof v === "name"`, the name a string literal: `Js.typeIs v
--| "string"`. What a module below `String` asks instead of comparing
--| `typeOf` with a string it may not write (`boundary.md` §4.2).
pub foreign pure typeIs : unused Value, borrowed name → fresh bool


--| `v instanceof C`: what a `catchIf` test asks of what was thrown,
--| `Js.instanceOf e (Js.global "TypeError")`.
pub foreign impure instanceOf : Value, Value → bool


--| `/pattern/flags`, a regular expression literal. Both arguments are
--| string literals; the flags are any of `d i m s u v`, each once (`g` and
--| `y` would make the value stateful). The pattern is not checked: one
--| JavaScript refuses stops the file loading, as a `RegExp` that throws
--| would stop the code that makes it. `Js.regExp "^\\d+$" ""` is
--| `/^\d+$/` (`backend.md` §4, *`Js.regExp` is a literal*).
pub foreign pure regExp : pattern, flags → Value


--| `globalThis.name`.
pub foreign impure global : name → Value


--| `o.name`, or `o[name]` when the name is not a literal identifier.
pub foreign impure get : Value, name → Value


--| `o.name = v`, a statement.
pub foreign impure set : Value, name, Value → ⊤


--| `o.name(a, b)`: the arguments are written as a list literal, one
--| JavaScript argument per element.
pub foreign impure call : Value, name, args → Value


--| `f(a, b)`: the arguments are written as a list literal.
pub foreign impure apply : Value, args → Value


--| One typed runtime protocol step (owner, 2026-11-01). The callback may
--| suspend: its raw result, including Task's sentinel, is returned for the
--| driver to consume. Its real type and callback summary are retained.
--| Core's fiber driver only; this does not turn suspension into a value
--| an application can hold. Unlike a host callback, `work` is not `sync`.
pub foreign impure invoke : once returned (a → b), aliased a → b


--| `new C(a, b)`: an object made by a constructor, the arguments written
--| as a list literal. `Js.construct (Js.global "Error") [ Js.from m ]` is
--| `new Error(m)`.
pub foreign impure construct : Value, args → Value


--| `[a, b]`: a new JavaScript array, its elements written as a list
--| literal.
pub foreign impure array : aliased elements → fresh Value


--| `{k1: v1, k2: v2}`: a new JavaScript object, its fields written as a
--| list literal of pairs, each key a string literal that is an identifier.
--| The keys are written in the order given and never renamed, so every
--| object one call makes has one shape; the values are made in the order
--| given, each of any type.
pub foreign pure object : aliased fields → fresh Value


--| `function () { const self = this; … }`: a function JavaScript calls as
--| a method, `o.m()`, its receiver handed to the lambda. Written with a
--| lambda, `Js.method λself → …`, the lambda's body is the function's and
--| no arrow is made. `sync`: JavaScript calls it and waits for nothing.
pub foreign pure method : sync (Value → a) → Value


--| `for (const x of xs) f(x)`: `f` for each element of an iterable, in
--| order. Written with a lambda, `Js.each xs λx → …`, the lambda's body
--| is the loop's body.
pub foreign impure each : escaped Value, many (Value → ⊤) → ⊤


--| `o[k]`: an element of an array, or of anything indexed by number.
pub foreign impure at : Value, index → Value


--| `o[k] = v`, a statement: an element of an array written.
pub foreign impure setAt : Value, index, Value → ⊤


--| `throw v`, a statement. Nothing after it runs.
pub foreign impure throw : escaped Value → fresh a


--| A mutable cell holding an `a`: `ref` makes one, `read` reads it and
--| `write` replaces what it holds. Where a cell never leaves the code that
--| made it — every use of its name is `read` or `write`, in its function or
--| in a closure made there — it is compiled to a plain `let`, a read to the
--| name and a write to an assignment; anywhere else it is `{ v }`
--| (`backend.md` §4, *A `Js.Ref` that does not escape is a `let`*).
pub foreign type Ref a


--| A new cell holding `v`.
pub foreign impure ref : escaped a → fresh Ref a


--| What the cell holds now.
pub foreign impure read : borrowed Ref a → unknown a


--| Make the cell hold `v`, a statement.
pub foreign impure write : borrowed Ref a, escaped a → ⊤


--| `try { body() } finally { cleanup() }`: the body's value, and the
--| cleanup run after the body however it ends — when it returns, and when
--| it throws, before the throw goes on. Written with lambdas,
--| `Js.finally (λ⊤ → …) λ⊤ → …`, each lambda's body is its block and
--| no function is made. Both are `sync`: a body that suspended would leave
--| the guard before its rest ran (`backend.md` §4, *`Js.finally` is `try …
--| finally`*).
pub foreign impure finally : once returned sync (⊤ → a), once sync (⊤ → ⊤) → a


--| `try { return body() } catch (e) { if (!test(e)) throw e; return
--| handler(e) }`: the body's value, or — when it throws something `test`
--| holds of — the handler's. Anything `test` does not hold of is thrown on
--| unchanged, so a wrapper catches exactly the failures its host API
--| documents and nothing else (`CLAUDE.md` rule 9): `Js.catchIf (λ() →
--| …) (λe → Js.instanceOf e (Js.global "URIError")) λ_ → Nothing`.
--| Written with lambdas, the body is the `try` block, the test and the
--| handler are the `catch` block, and no function is made. All three are
--| `sync`, as `finally`'s arguments are (`backend.md` §4, *`Js.catchIf`
--| is `try … catch`*).
pub foreign impure catchIf :
      once returned sync (⊤ → a)
    , once sync (Value → bool)
    , once returned sync (Value → a)
    → a


--| The body's value, and a promise: that computing it is total, throws
--| nothing and changes nothing an observer can see — what `foreign pure`
--| promises of a sibling's function, made of beni written over `Js`, whose
--| reads and calls the checker otherwise takes for `impure` (`String.length`
--| is `Js.get` of `Array.from`'s `length`, and pure). Unchecked, like every
--| `Js` declaration: the caller answers for it. Written with a lambda,
--| `Js.pure λ⊤ → …` is the lambda's body and no function is made
--| (`backend.md` §4, *`Js.pure` is its body*).
pub foreign pure pure : once returned sync (⊤ → a) → a


--| `True` in a development build and `False` under `--release`. An `if`
--| on it keeps only the branch the build takes: the other is neither
--| written nor reached, so a declaration only it names costs the other
--| build nothing — a development crash screen ships in no release build
--| (`backend.md` §4, *`Js.development` is the build's mode*). A function
--| of `⊤` because a `foreign` value that is not one may not be
--| polymorphic, and this module names no `Bool`.
pub foreign pure development : ⊤ → bool


--| `True` under the hidden build flag `--test-hooks` and `False` otherwise,
--| as `development` is true in a development build: an `if` on it keeps
--| only the branch the build takes, so a platform's test hooks — what a
--| corpus fixture drives time with (`boundary.md` §9.12 (k)) — ship in no
--| build that does not ask for them (`backend.md` §4, *`Js.testHooks` is
--| the build's test hooks*).
pub foreign pure testHooks : ⊤ → bool


--| Whether `f`, a function, may suspend: the answer the checker infers for
--| it where this is called, written in place as `true` or `false`. In a
--| declaration whose argument is the one asked about, that is a question
--| about each use: the declaration gets a direct body where the answer is
--| `false` and a suspendable one where it is `true`, and an `if` on it
--| keeps only the branch each body takes — so what only the other branch
--| needs costs a program that never takes it nothing (`backend.md` §4,
--| *`Js.maySuspend` is the body's answer*).
pub foreign pure maySuspend : f → bool


--| The identity of `k`'s type: a string the compiler writes where this is
--| called. Development names the type with its module and every argument;
--| release linking uses private atom ids in the same grammar. Thus two
--| calls give equal strings exactly when their types are one. Where `k` is
--| a type variable, the string is built from the identity the declaration's
--| own callers hand it, which the compiler supplies with nothing written.
--| `x` is evaluated and not read, and so is `o`, any `Order` — `EQ` — which
--| names the type `compare` answers, since this module names no core type.
--| What `Hosted.key` tells the types of two keys apart with (`boundary.md`
--| §9.8.3; `static-dispatch-spike.md` §8.6).
pub foreign pure fingerprint : unused k, unused order → fresh name
    where k.compare : k, k → order


--| The body's value, and the opposite promise to `pure`'s: that it may be
--| the fiber runtime's sentinel, so a call of it may suspend. What `Task`
--| returns its sentinel with, written in beni; nothing else needs it.
--| Unchecked, like every `Js` declaration. Written with a lambda,
--| `Js.suspending λ⊤ → …` is the lambda's body and a suspension point
--| where it is not in tail position (`backend.md` §4, *`Js.suspending` is
--| its body*).
pub foreign suspends suspending : once returned sync (⊤ → a) → a


--| A typed mutable record. Core and platform runtimes only.
pub cell foreign type Cell record


--| A fresh cell with the record's fields. A literal is written in place.
pub foreign impure cell : aliased record → fresh Cell record


--| Read one field, selected with a literal accessor such as `.state`.
pub foreign impure cellRead : cellread Cell record, unused sync (record → a) → lent a


--| Store a value in one field. The literal selector is not invoked.
pub foreign impure cellWrite :
      cellwrite Cell record
    , consumed cellstored a
    , unused sync (record → a)
    → ⊤


--| An optional value represented by the private sentinel or its payload.
pub foreign type Opt payload


--| The empty slot. Uses the one private sentinel; no per-slot allocation.
pub foreign pure none : unused ⊤ → fresh Opt payload


--| A present slot. Nested empty slots retain their exact depth.
pub foreign pure some : returned payload → Opt payload


--| Call exactly one sync branch. The present branch receives a safe payload.
pub foreign impure optMatch :
      escaped Opt payload
    , once returned sync (⊤ → b)
    , once returned sync (payload → b)
    → b


--| Read the slot, or use its typed default when empty.
pub foreign pure optWithDefault : returned Opt payload, returned payload → payload


-- The compiler-owned absence identity, private to the runtime protocol.
foreign pure optNone : Value


--| Typed array capacity while core fills its elements.
pub sequence foreign type Builder a
